  "question": {
    "text": "The Lewis acid character of boron tri halides follows the order:"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>BBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>BBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>BBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The strength of the acidic character of boron trihalides&nbsp;depends upon&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi></math>&nbsp;back bonding.&nbsp;In boron trihalides,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi></math>&nbsp;-back bonding occurs due to empty orbital of boron and filled orbitals of halogens.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0021_64d04c23dad0.png\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 266px;\" /></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi></math>&nbsp;back bonding is shown maximum by<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>, as the size of B and F are small and comparatively same. Due to this effect tendency of accepting lone-pair of electrons of boron decreases as the size of halogen increases. The order of size of halogens are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Cl</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Br</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> . Thus, acidic nature is in order&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>BBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>.</p>"
  }
